.\" SPDX-License-Identifier: BSD-3-Clause
.Dd September 29, 2026
.Dt SENDMSG 2
.Os OxideBSD
.Sh NAME
.Nm send ,
.Nm sendto ,
.Nm sendmsg
.Nd send a message on a socket
.Sh LIBRARY
.Lb libc
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In sys/socket.h
.Ft ssize_t
.Fn send "int s" "const void *buf" "size_t len" "int flags"
.Ft ssize_t
.Fo sendto
.Fa "int s"
.Fa "const void *buf"
.Fa "size_t len"
.Fa "int flags"
.Fa "const struct sockaddr *to"
.Fa "socklen_t tolen"
.Fc
.Ft ssize_t
.Fn sendmsg "int s" "const struct msghdr *msg" "int flags"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
These calls send data on the socket
.Fa s .
The
.Fn sendmsg
system call does the work; the C library implements
.Fn send
and
.Fn sendto
over it, as the native system-call interface passes at most four
arguments.
.Pp
.Fn send
sends
.Fa len
bytes from
.Fa buf
on a connected socket, or to a datagram socket's default destination.
.Fn sendto
sends them to the address
.Fa to ,
of
.Fa tolen
bytes, or behaves as
.Fn send
if
.Fa to
is
.Dv NULL .
.Pp
.Fn sendmsg
takes everything from a
.Vt struct msghdr :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
struct msghdr {
	void		*msg_name;	/* destination address, or NULL */
	socklen_t	 msg_namelen;	/* its length */
	struct iovec	*msg_iov;	/* data to send */
	int		 msg_iovlen;	/* elements in msg_iov */
	void		*msg_control;	/* control data */
	socklen_t	 msg_controllen; /* its length */
	int		 msg_flags;	/* not used when sending */
};
.Ed
.Pp
The data is gathered from the
.Fa msg_iovlen
buffers of
.Fa msg_iov ,
at most
.Dv IOV_MAX
(1024) of them.
Control data is a series of
.Vt struct cmsghdr ,
each built with the
.Fn CMSG_*
macros of
.In sys/socket.h ,
at most 4096 bytes in all; only local sockets accept any, and
.Xr unix 4
describes the types
.Pq Dv SCM_RIGHTS , SCM_CREDS , SCM_CREDENTIALS .
Control data goes with the first byte of the data it was sent with.
.Pp
On a datagram or sequenced-packet socket the data is sent as one message,
whole or not at all.
On a stream socket as much is sent as fits; a blocking send waits for room
until everything is sent, and returns what was sent so far if it is then
interrupted, times out, or fails.
.Pp
A blocking send waits at most as long as the socket's
.Dv SO_SNDTIMEO
option says, if it is set.
.Pp
The
.Fa flags
are the OR of none or more of:
.Bl -tag -width MSG_NOSIGNAL
.It Dv MSG_DONTWAIT
do not block: fail with
.Er EAGAIN
instead, as if the socket had
.Dv O_NONBLOCK .
.It Dv MSG_EOR
end the record
.Pq Dv SOCK_SEQPACKET No only ; every send ends one anyway .
.It Dv MSG_NOSIGNAL
do not raise
.Dv SIGPIPE
when the send fails with
.Er EPIPE .
.El
.Pp
Any other flag, including
.Dv MSG_OOB ,
makes the call fail with
.Er EOPNOTSUPP ;
flags are never ignored.
.Pp
A send that fails with
.Er EPIPE
raises
.Dv SIGPIPE
in the calling thread unless
.Dv MSG_NOSIGNAL
is given or the socket's
.Dv SO_NOSIGPIPE
option is set.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
These calls return the number of bytes sent.
Otherwise they return \-1 and set
.Va errno .
.Sh ERRORS
.Bl -tag -width Er
.It Bq Er EACCES
Write permission is denied on the socket file named by
.Fa to .
.It Bq Er EAGAIN
The socket is non-blocking or
.Dv MSG_DONTWAIT
was given, and the send would block; or
.Dv SO_SNDTIMEO
expired before anything was sent.
.It Bq Er EBADF
.Fa s
is not an open descriptor, or a descriptor in an
.Dv SCM_RIGHTS
message is not open.
.It Bq Er ECONNREFUSED
No socket is bound to the local name
.Fa to .
.It Bq Er EDESTADDRREQ
No address was given, and the socket is a UDP socket without a default
destination or a raw socket.
.It Bq Er EHOSTUNREACH
The IPv4 destination cannot be reached.
.It Bq Er EINTR
A signal interrupted the call before anything was sent.
.It Bq Er EINVAL
.Fa msg
is
.Dv NULL ,
.Fa msg_controllen
is more than 4096, an
.Fa iov_len
is too large, a buffer pointer is
.Dv NULL
with a length that is not zero, the address is malformed, or the control
data is malformed.
.It Bq Er EISCONN
An address was given on a connected UDP socket, or on a connected local
stream or sequenced-packet socket.
A TCP socket ignores the address.
.It Bq Er EMSGSIZE
.Fa msg_iovlen
is more than
.Dv IOV_MAX ,
or the message is too large to send in one piece.
.It Bq Er ENOBUFS
The receiving local datagram socket has no room for the message.
.It Bq Er ENOTCONN
The socket is not connected, or a local datagram socket's default
destination has closed.
.It Bq Er ENOTSOCK
.Fa s
is not a socket.
.It Bq Er EOPNOTSUPP
A flag other than those above was given,
.Dv MSG_EOR
was given on a socket that is not
.Dv SOCK_SEQPACKET ,
control data was given on a socket that is not local, or an address was
given on an unconnected local stream or sequenced-packet socket.
.It Bq Er EPERM
An
.Dv SCM_CREDENTIALS
message holds credentials other than the sender's.
.It Bq Er EPIPE
The socket's write side is shut down, or the connection's peer has gone.
.It Bq Er EPROTOTYPE
The local socket named by
.Fa to
is not a datagram socket.
.It Bq Er ETOOMANYREFS
Too many descriptions are in flight; see
.Xr unix 4 .
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr recvmsg 2 ,
.Xr socket 2 ,
.Xr write 2 ,
.Xr unix 4
.Sh STANDARDS
These calls conform to
.St -p1003.1-2008 .
.Sh HISTORY
The
.Fn send ,
.Fn sendto
and
.Fn sendmsg
system calls first appeared in
.Bx 4.2 .
In OxideBSD
.Fn send
and
.Fn sendto
are library functions over
.Fn sendmsg ,
system call 577; the former
.Fn sendto
system call, 142, is retired and fails with
.Er ENOSYS .
